.TH postar 1  "postar 0.1.0" 
.SH NAME
postar \- A local email filtering service
.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBpostar\fR [\fB\-c\fR|\fB\-\-config\fR] [\fB\-r\fR|\fB\-\-rules\fR] [\fB\-\-log\fR] [\fB\-s\fR|\fB\-\-server\fR] [\fB\-\-db\fR] [\fB\-\-polling\-delay\fR] [\fB\-\-check\fR] [\fB\-\-dry\-run\-remote\fR] [\fB\-\-dry\-run\-local\fR] [\fB\-h\fR|\fB\-\-help\fR] [\fB\-V\fR|\fB\-\-version\fR] [\fIsubcommands\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.PP
This is a local email filtering program that watches for emails in the configured mailboxes and matches 
the rules to emails and executes certain actions.
.PP
There are two main files you need to pay attention to: the config file and the rules file.
.PP
The config file is a simple TOML file which configures some global options that influence the programs
execution. It is located at $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/postar/config.toml by default. For the complete reference
of configuration options, refer to https://github.com/filiptrplan/postar?tab=readme\-ov\-file#toml\-configuration\-reference.
.PP
The rules file is written using a bespoke DSL and defines the rules to match against and the actions
executed when those rules are matched. It is located at $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/postar/rules.ptar by default.
For a complete reference and a short tutorial see https://github.com/filiptrplan/postar?tab=readme\-ov\-file#rule\-dsl.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
\fB\-c\fR, \fB\-\-config\fR \fI<CONFIG>\fR
Path to the TOML config file.

This specifies things like default flags and all the connection details.

Configuration reference: https://github.com/filiptrplan/postar?tab=readme\-ov\-file#toml\-configuration\-reference
.TP
\fB\-r\fR, \fB\-\-rules\fR \fI<RULES>\fR
Path to the PTAR rules file.

This specifies how the emails should be filtered and which actions should be executed upon rule match.

Rule reference: https://github.com/filiptrplan/postar?tab=readme\-ov\-file#rule\-dsl
.TP
\fB\-\-log\fR \fI<LOG>\fR [default: info]
The logging level
.br

.br
[\fIpossible values: \fRoff, error, warn, info, debug, trace]
.TP
\fB\-s\fR, \fB\-\-server\fR \fI<SERVER>\fR
The server that postar connects to.

It can be either specified in the config file by settings the default option to true or by passing in this flag.
.TP
\fB\-\-db\fR \fI<DB>\fR
Path to the persistent database. Ordinary users should not change this option
.TP
\fB\-\-polling\-delay\fR \fI<POLLING_DELAY>\fR
The polling delay when using the polling method for inboxes.

This is relevant when the IDLE capability for IMAP inboxes is not available so the program must poll. This can be either specified as a flag or in the config file.
.TP
\fB\-\-check\fR
Check whether the configuration is valid
.TP
\fB\-\-dry\-run\-remote\fR
Perform a dry run on the most recent 10 messages
.TP
\fB\-\-dry\-run\-local\fR \fI<DRY_RUN_LOCAL>\fR
Peform a dry run on messages stored locally on disk.

The argument for this option is a folder containing .eml files that the dry run will be pefromed on
.TP
\fB\-h\fR, \fB\-\-help\fR
Print help (see a summary with \*(Aq\-h\*(Aq)
.TP
\fB\-V\fR, \fB\-\-version\fR
Print version
.SH SUBCOMMANDS
.TP
postar\-completions(1)
Outputs shell completions to stdout.
.TP
postar\-init(1)
Initializes the configuration files
.TP
postar\-list\-folders(1)
Lists all the folders for a specific mailbox
.SH VERSION
v0.1.0
